    skillId: "ai-seo",
    path: "references/youtube-ai-citations.md",
    content: `# YouTube Videos That Get Cited by AI\r
\r
YouTube is one of the most-cited third-party surfaces in AI answers — Google AI Overviews and Gemini cite it heavily, and ChatGPT/Perplexity lift from it for how-to queries. The core insight that changes how you produce for it:\r
\r
**Models don't watch your video. They read everything around it.** The citation is earned by the text layer — title, transcript, captions, chapters, description, and comments — not the footage. A mediocre-looking video with a clean, structured text layer beats a beautiful one that's opaque to a crawler.\r
\r
## The anatomy\r
\r
Work through these in order of leverage:\r
\r
### 1. The transcript (the real content)\r
\r
This is what the model actually reads. Optimize the *spoken words*:\r
\r
- **Answer questions in complete, liftable sentences.** "The five steps to create an SOP are…" extracts cleanly; a rambling answer spread across three tangents doesn't.\r
- Script or outline the key answers before recording so each core question gets a clear, structured spoken answer in one place.\r
- Say the important terms out loud — the product name, the category, the entities you want associated. If it's only on a slide, the model may never see it.\r
\r
### 2. Accurate captions\r
\r
Auto-captions are messy — misheard product names, no punctuation, broken sentences — and messy captions are what the model reads if you don't fix them. Upload cleaned captions (or at minimum correct the auto-generated ones). This is the cheapest fix on the list.\r
\r
### 3. A question-shaped title\r
\r
Models match the title against the user's prompt. "How to Create SOPs That Scale Your Business" beats a clever title every time. Front-load the question or task; save the branding for the channel.\r
\r
### 4. Chapters and timestamps\r
\r
Chapters let the model (and viewers) jump to the exact answer. Structure = extractability: each chapter title is another labeled, liftable claim about what the video covers. Match chapter titles to the sub-questions people actually ask.\r
\r
### 5. A keyword-rich, structured description\r
\r
Restate the video's key points *as text* in the description — a short summary, then a bulleted list of what's covered, then resource links. This reinforces the topic and entities in plain crawlable text and gives the model a second, cleaner copy of the answer.\r
\r
### 6. A pinned comment with the summary\r
\r
An extra liftable text block: pin a comment with the core answer in numbered steps plus the key links. It's indexed, it's structured, and it survives even when viewers never open the description.\r
\r
### 7. Thumbnail and engagement\r
\r
Engagement isn't read directly by LLMs, but it drives the watch signals that lift YouTube ranking — and YouTube ranking feeds what AI systems surface and cite. The thumbnail's job is the click; the text layer's job is the citation.\r
\r
## Publishing checklist\r
\r
- [ ] Title is question- or task-shaped and matches a real query\r
- [ ] Key answers spoken as complete, structured statements\r
- [ ] Captions uploaded or corrected (product names spelled right)\r
- [ ] Chapters added, titled by sub-question\r
- [ ] Description restates the key points in text with a bulleted breakdown\r
- [ ] Pinned comment carries the summary + links\r
- [ ] Important entities (brand, category, product) spoken *and* written\r
\r
## Field notes (Sep 2026)\r
\r
- **The citation bar can be low.** In a live check, an 8-day-old video with under 100 views was the first source cited in Google's AI Overview for "best email marketing software." Fresh, well-labeled videos on head terms are worth testing.\r
- **Ungate your demos.** Post product demos publicly with keyword-first titles ("[Category] software demo – [Brand]") instead of locking them behind a form.\r
- **Sponsored creator videos are hard to attribute.** Practitioners quote $20–40K per sponsored video and no reliable way to prove the return. Budget them with an attribution plan (the self-reported field in [citations-vs-recommendations.md](citations-vs-recommendations.md)) or not at all.\r
\r
## Related\r
\r
- The same "models read the text layer" logic applies to podcasts: episodes get transcribed and show notes get published, so podcast guesting is earned media that compounds in AI answers — see the \`public-relations\` skill's podcast guest prep reference.\r
- For producing the videos themselves, see the \`video\` skill.\r
\r
---\r
\r
*Anatomy pattern from Ross Simmonds / Foundation Inc. ("The Anatomy of a YouTube Video AI Cites," 2026), distilled and extended with credit.*\r
`,
